{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15+v3-2026-10-04+upright-2026-10-04+v3-2026-10-04+v3-2026-10-06+v3-2026-10-06+qa-fixes-2026-10-08","concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/mgf-location-scale.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/mgf-location-scale.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ be a random variable with MGF $M_X$, and let $a$ and $b$ be real constants. Which identity gives the MGF of $a+bX$ wherever $M_X(bt)$ is finite?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{bt}M_X(at)$","$a+b\\,M_X(t)$","$\\mathrm{e}^{at}M_X(bt)$","$\\mathrm{e}^{at}\\big(M_X(t)\\big)^b$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5f6a7750c87354f9ef45801c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The MGF of $X$ is finite exactly when $-7<t<11$. After the shift to $13+X$, on which set of $t$ is the new MGF finite?","options":["$6<t<24$","$-7<t<11$","$-20<t<-2$","$-\\tfrac7{13}<t<\\tfrac{11}{13}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b8148aea21a56eb81afa73a7","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ be a random variable whose MGF is finite near zero. Which identity always gives the MGF of $-X$ near zero?","options":["$M_{-X}(t)=\\mathrm{e}^{-t}M_X(t)$","$M_{-X}(t)=1/M_X(t)$","$M_{-X}(t)=M_X(t)$","$M_{-X}(t)=M_X(-t)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c39745aa79967491bc38d2c0","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ be a random variable and $a$ a real constant. What is $M_{a+0X}(t)$?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{at}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{at}M_X(t)$","$M_X(at)$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ccad55a676b332def69ec8ee","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $M_X(t)$ is finite exactly for $t<1$. For which $t$ is the MGF of $2X$ finite?","options":["$t<2$","$t<\\tfrac12$","$t<1$","All real $t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"55b0681dc4ce53a12718815e","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A count $X\\sim\\operatorname{Pois}(2)$ has $M_X(t)=\\exp(2(\\mathrm{e}^t-1))$. Which function is the MGF of the tripled count $3X$?","options":["$\\exp(6(\\mathrm{e}^t-1))$","$3\\exp(2(\\mathrm{e}^t-1))$","$\\exp(2(3\\mathrm{e}^t-1))$","$\\exp(2(\\mathrm{e}^{3t}-1))$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"78691aacca27b444ef376093","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"An exponential waiting time has $M_X(t)=\\dfrac1{1-t}$ for $t<1$. Find the MGF of the centred time $X-1$.","options":["$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{-t}}{1-t}$","$\\dfrac1{1-t}-1$","$\\dfrac1{2-t}$","$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{t}}{1-t}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"908e291e4efab6a045d17a3b","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $M_Z(t)=\\mathrm{e}^{t^2/2}$. What is the MGF of $-1+3Z$?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{-t+3t^2/2}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{3t-t^2/2}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-t+9t^2/2}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-t+9t^2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"139d0fdd5a9801c4e30223c9","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A fair coin gives $X\\sim\\operatorname{Bern}(\\tfrac12)$ with $M_X(t)=\\tfrac12(1+\\mathrm{e}^t)$. What is the MGF of the $\\pm1$ payoff $Y=2X-1$?","options":["$\\tfrac12(1+\\mathrm{e}^{2t})$","$\\tfrac12(\\mathrm{e}^{t}+\\mathrm{e}^{-t})$","$(1+\\mathrm{e}^t)-1$","$\\tfrac12(\\mathrm{e}^{2t}+\\mathrm{e}^{-t})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9c044504c9efbf253e6af2c0","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X$ have mean zero, variance one and an MGF finite near zero. A variable $Y$ has MGF $\\mathrm{e}^{4t}M_X(3t)$ near zero. Which function of $X$ has this MGF?","options":["$4+3X$","$3+4X$","$4+\\tfrac13X$","$12X$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c62028b5af65488cc4e490b6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a real constant $a$ and a random variable $X$ with MGF finite near zero, the step $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{e}^{t(a+X)}]=\\mathrm{e}^{at}\\,\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{e}^{tX}]$ needs no independence assumption. Why?","options":["The exponential of a sum is the sum of the exponentials","Expectation factors over every product of random variables","Taking expectation removes the location shift $a$","The factor $\\mathrm{e}^{at}$ is the same number on every outcome"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7bbfbb497b341fb8b1077d84","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X$ have mean $\\mu$, standard deviation $\\sigma>0$ and an MGF finite near zero. For $Z=(X-\\mu)/\\sigma$, which expression is $M_Z(t)$ wherever $M_X(t/\\sigma)$ is finite?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{-\\mu t}M_X(t/\\sigma)$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-\\mu t/\\sigma}M_X(\\sigma t)$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-\\mu t/\\sigma}M_X(t/\\sigma)$","$\\dfrac{M_X(t)-\\mu}\\sigma$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8ae81e61690ba51a7722f099","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"$X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has $M_X(t)=\\mathrm{e}^{\\mu t+\\sigma^2t^2/2}$. With $b\\ne0$, the rule and uniqueness of MGFs show that $a+bX$ has which law?","options":["$\\mathcal{N}(a+b\\mu,\\ b^2\\sigma^2)$","$\\mathcal{N}(a+b\\mu,\\ b\\sigma^2)$","$\\mathcal{N}(b\\mu,\\ a+b^2\\sigma^2)$","$\\mathcal{N}(a+b\\mu,\\ a^2+b^2\\sigma^2)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"be3eda8d860e910f54af1711","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Stretch and shift $X\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}(0,1)$, whose MGF is $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^t-1}t$ for $t\\ne0$, into $Y=2X-1$. Which MGF does $Y$ have for $t\\ne0$?","options":["$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{2t}-1}{2t}$","$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{t}-\\mathrm{e}^{-t}}{2t}$","$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{t}-\\mathrm{e}^{-t}}{t}$","$\\dfrac{2(\\mathrm{e}^{t}-1)}t-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"970f056fad56d079a73dd627","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X$ have an MGF finite near zero, and let $a$ and $b$ be real constants. Using $M_X'(0)=\\mathbb{E}[X]$, differentiate $M_{a+bX}(t)=\\mathrm{e}^{at}M_X(bt)$ at zero. Which rule follows?","options":["$\\mathbb{E}[a+bX]=b\\,\\mathbb{E}[X]$","$\\mathbb{E}[a+bX]=ab\\,\\mathbb{E}[X]$","$\\mathbb{E}[a+bX]=a\\,\\mathbb{E}[X]+b$","$\\mathbb{E}[a+bX]=a+b\\,\\mathbb{E}[X]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3974ad2f926b476313779548","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"$X$ has $M_X(t)=\\tfrac13\\mathrm{e}^{-t}+\\tfrac23\\mathrm{e}^{2t}$. Using the MGF of $Y=1-X$, what is $\\mathbb{P}(Y=2)$?","options":["$\\tfrac23$","$0$","$\\tfrac13$","$\\tfrac12$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f75b3a23a5d93504daf282c8","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a$ and $b\\ne0$ be real constants and $Y=a+bX$. Suppose $M_Y$ is finite for every $|t|<\\delta$, where $\\delta>0$. Which identity and guaranteed finite neighbourhood follow for $M_X(s)$?","options":["$M_X(s)=\\mathrm{e}^{-as}M_Y(bs)$, finite for $|s|<\\delta/|b|$","$M_X(s)=\\mathrm{e}^{-as/b}M_Y(s/b)$, finite for $|s|<|b|\\delta$","$M_X(s)=\\mathrm{e}^{as/b}M_Y(s/b)$, finite for $|s|<|b|\\delta$","$M_X(s)=\\mathrm{e}^{-as/b}M_Y(s/b)$, finite for $|s|<\\delta/|b|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d0b9ecd841146f2dd0300a75","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $X$ and $-X$ have the same distribution and $M_X$ is finite near zero. Using $M_X^{(n)}(0)=\\mathbb{E}[X^n]$ for positive integers $n$, which pair of consequences follows from the negative-scale rule?","options":["$M_X(t)=M_X(-t)$ near zero; all odd moments vanish.","$M_X(t)=1$ near zero; all positive-order moments vanish.","$M_X(t)=-M_X(-t)$ near zero; all even moments vanish.","$M_X(t)M_X(-t)=1$ near zero; the variance vanishes."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d7699b9a3f912df0a8ffe79a","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A random variable $X$ has an MGF finite near zero, with $M_X'(0)=6$ and $M_X''(0)=46$. Set $Y=7-2X$. Using the affine MGF rule and $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=M_Y''(0)-(M_Y'(0))^2$, what is $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$?","options":["$20$","$46$","$40$","$89$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c8b1f91a7e5587a8285e7b0c","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_mgf_location_scale_13b1feb8_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A random variable $X$ has $M_X(s)<\\infty$ exactly for $-7\\le s<11$. For which $t$ is the MGF of $Y=8-4X$ finite?","options":["$-\\tfrac74\\le t<\\tfrac{11}4$","$-\\tfrac{11}4\\le t<\\tfrac74$","$-44<t\\le28$","$-\\tfrac{11}4<t\\le\\tfrac74$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bdb80576c30d2f11d4abd2f4","time_limit":40}]}}